WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
How many stories really
in the world are there?

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:05.000
I think there's only
a handful of stories

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.960
as a general,
global, universal idea.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.040
There's only a handful

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.720
because it's based on
experiences that are shared.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.640
But then you get specific

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.080
because there's
the mechanics

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.400
of how things play out
that's different.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.960
Hello, everyone! My name's
Matthias Schweighöfer.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.480
Hi, I'm Zack Snyder.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.000
I am
Nosipho Dumisa-Ngoasheng.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.400
-I'm Peter Thorwarth.
-My name's Adam Price.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.520
-I'm Shinsuke Sato.
-I'm Deborah Feldman.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.960
-I'm Álex Pina.
-Hi, I'm Harlan Coben.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.160
The three act structures
is great

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.120
because you have a start,
you have a middle,

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.400
and you have an end.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.200
Three act structure
is very important.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.000
Personally, for me,
that's kind of what

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.200
I've gravitated towards

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.360
and always understood
really well.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.960
I think it also gives you
great markers

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.560
for
is this still interesting

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.640
or have we gotten boring

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.280
and bland
and things seem the same.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.160
I kind of think
that I end up with

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.960
a slight three
and a half-act,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.800
or almost four-act
basic structure.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.800
I don't like
any kind of structure,

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.320
at least consciously
thinking about it.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.600
We very much concentrate on
the whole episode

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.320
and find our way

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.600
in a natural structure
for the episodes.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:14.920
Back in the day, my mindset

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.800
was that I had to have
a three act structure.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.360
So I was very intentional

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.600
on dividing it
into three acts.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.920
But the more I wrote,

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.480
the less I worried
about the structure.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.480
The key to any protagonist
is they have to be real.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:41.720
Has to be that character,

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:42.880
that when
you’re sitting at a bar,

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.280
you kind of wanna overhear
the conversation a little.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.000
He must have a secret

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.880
and he has to have something
that is really touching.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:51.800
In the end,

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.200
writing is a lot like
designing characters.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.000
We are like psychologists.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.920
My favourite stuff
to work on

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.320
is always why this character
is like a great hacker,

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.440
or why this character
is a great get-away driver.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.280
The perspective
we have on a character

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.760
at the opening of a film,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.360
and then who they are
at the end of the film,

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.840
and the journey
that they go on

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.800
to become,
hopefully, a hero,

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.840
or, at least,
a realised character

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.080
with hopes
and dreams and aspirations

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.680
and ability to love or hate
or whatever it is.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.080
What you learn

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.120
in the first third
of Unorthodox

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.920
is an internal process
of questioning and doubting

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.040
that occurs in the mind
of the protagonist.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.200
You want a protagonist
to have some problem.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.040
I think
if you have a really, like,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.600
perfect protagonist
who is perfect

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.240
and everyone likes
and has no flaws,

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.520
then there's
no real interesting story.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.960
The best twists
come really from the heart.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.240
You can have the most
expensive car in the world,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.440
but if it doesn't have fuel,
it's not going any place.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.760
This is true of all fiction
you've ever done,

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.040
is your hero travels
down the road of life

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.400
and you've got to put
speed bumps and pitfalls

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:04.560
and detours...

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:05.640
Being a writer

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:11.280
that works so much
in the 100% realistic genre,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.120
it was a joy for me,
actually,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.120
to go into
the supernatural genre

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.400
and all of a sudden,

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.480
discussing
can this character fly?

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.480
And it was amazing

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.080
to have
all these possibilities

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.200
on your table.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:33.720
As a director,

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.880
there are layers of myself
that sometimes get added

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.360
to the character I create.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:45.440
Sometimes,
my own characteristics

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:52.360
are aspects I end up adding
into the characters.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:56.400
I think I went back
into the past

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:59.960
and I plucked episodes
from my life,

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.000
and I sorted them

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.760
chronologically
next to each other

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.480
in order to build
some kind of art.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:08.400
I can't say

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.560
that the story isn't any way
based on my life.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.040
Blood &amp; Water
is not based on my life,

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.160
it is not based on any
one specific person's life.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.520
What I'm trying to write

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.120
is a story
that will be universal.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.080
And I've always found
the more specific the local,

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:25.560
the more universal
the appeal.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.280
The themes are universal.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.680
Love, redemption,

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:31.880
family, friendship,
relationships, hope.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.920
Those things are universal.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:34.840
I never believed

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.880
that Blood Red Sky was just
for, again, Germany,

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.840
so it should be
a German movie

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.400
and I had the idea:

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:42.880
what can be
more international

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.640
than
a trans-Atlantic flight?

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.720
It's about being specific
in your context,

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:48.640
but being global

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.840
in your universal idea
and story.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.080
The story, itself,
is coming-of-age young girl

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.680
trying to find
her missing sister.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.080
The idea of coming-of-age
and being in high school,

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.120
everyone
can understand that.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.640
That's the globe part of it.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.200
How will I write
so that people believe me?

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.640
And how will I write it
so that they understand me?

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.400
I think
it's a natural thing to feel

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.040
when you sit down
to write any book,

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.240
but especially a memoir.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.440
I think, as a writer,
it's extremely important

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.440
to remind yourself,
at all times,

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.000
that you cannot write
for everyone,

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.600
you cannot write a story
for 190 countries.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:23.280
Because, I think,

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.040
if you try to write
for everyone

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.600
you will end up writing
for no one.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.440
I remember talking
to a Japanese reporter,

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:33.280
when the Japanese edition
of Unorthodox

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:34.680
was released as a book,

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:37.920
and she was telling me,
"Do you know that the story

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.440
of every
Japanese women's life

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.080
is unorthodox?"
She started to cry.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.360
It's almost like
instead of this just being

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:48.520
my weird,
unusual life experience,

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.120
it's now part of
a universal spectrum

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:52.800
of the experiences of women.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.760
There is no reason

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.600
for a woman
to experience shame

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.920
as a result of
this very common

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.600
lived bodied experience.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.600
And that, to me,

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.040
is what I think
touched a nerve

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.200
for women
all over the world.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.560
On a business level,

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.480
Alice in Borderland
was created

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:13.320
with the Japanese market
in mind.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.360
But on a personal
and emotional level,

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.360
in became an opportunity
to share Japan's atmosphere

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:25.080
and its domestic feeling
and ambience.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:26.960
I always hoped
an international audience

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:27.880
who didn't know Japan,

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.760
would get excited
or be deeply moved by it.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.040
When we made the decision

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:36.080
that we do a whole film
about different nations,

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.800
and different people
from everywhere in Europe,

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.320
it became
like a really colourful film

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.720
and I love that
because language barriers,

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.360
fantastic
for a great sense of humour.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:47.720
Close your eyes.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.400
In the end, I always say
the writer is like a cook.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:53.680
Each country
has its own recipe,

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.760
but from the same
universal ingredients.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.560
It's okay for it to be bad.
Just write something.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:02.200
I become
completely obsessed.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.840
I think about it
night and day,

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.600
all of the time.
I wake up thinking about it,

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.640
I'm a distracted friend
or guest, you know,

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.520
if you're talking to--
my kids are used to it.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:11.440
Like, all of a sudden,

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:12.520
I'll start giving
one of these

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:13.440
and the kids will go,

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.520
"Ah, Dad's gone off
to la-la land again."

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.480
I would rewrite
then throw it away

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:19.040
and rewrite
and throw it away

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.440
over and over,

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.280
to get to a point
of satisfaction.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:25.680
Even the worst things,
things people would be like,

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:26.960
"This is a terrible idea."

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:28.800
It's fine,
'cause from a terrible idea,

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:30.240
something great
may come out.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.840
When I have, like,
a little bit of a block,

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:34.600
I just write notes to myself

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.760
about what I want
to have happen in the scene,

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.280
and, like,
what needs to happen.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:40.360
It can get you
jumpstarted a little bit.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.240
I think work is a journey.

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:44.720
What you do
and what you learn.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.720
Hence, you learn
from your mistakes.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.040
You want to write something

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.520
you're passionate about,
you know?

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.840
Like, your story,
whatever it is,

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:54.280
whatever
keeps you up at night.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:57.200
Writing is pretty arduous,
it requires discipline,

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:01.320
and I think that the passion
for the thing,

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:06.400
is great fuel
to allow you to do the work

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.320
of sitting and, you know,
putting pen to paper

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:10.920
or fingers to keyboard.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.440
-Any questions?
-No.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:14.160
Yes. Many. Hundreds.

